ere  the ventral striatum and the amygdala um  very sensitive to reward including  social rewards of  finding and social interaction rewarding  and what this is saying is that to the  extent that these brain regions are  active and connected in terms of their  activity  the child will do better that's what  this graph is showing  in pivotal response training now a lot  of times  when i talk about this data  if there are clinicians in the audience  they'll kind of say  yeah so you just told me that if a child  can notice social information and pay  attention to it  and if they can regulate their behavior  and  interact with me and if they find it  rewarding  they'll do well in the the intervention  that you're talking about and that seems  kind of obvious  it's like yes but  i'm doing two things that that  that are deeper than that simple  observation  one is these are quantitative measures  that vary by child of levels of  those constructs that we just mentioned  in passing  that really don't have  a reliable and sensitive measure out  there  in nature that can be used by a  clinician to make a decision  and in fact when we looked at all of our  behavioral data  and all of our clinician ratings  we found that that data in contrast to  the brain data  just did a terrible job of predicting  which kids  would benefit from the treatment and one  of the simplest things we did was ask  expert clinicians do you think this  child is a good candidate for this  treatment can you estimate how well  they'll respond  and there's some there's funny things  that happen within cognitive  cognitive psychology talks about these  extensively  that when you're an expert in something  it becomes it can become very  challenging for certain  reasons because of cognitive biases  to make a judgment about a particular  case  and and so actually our imaging  approach was doing a much better job  than even  some of the very best clinicians in the  country when they reviewed the files and  saw  videos of these kids and sometimes met  them in person  being able to predict so we're doing a  very good job  of predicting and that's fine  but we were also showing quite  literally parts of the brain that if  they were  active and connected during the  intervention  if we ramped them up prior to the  intervention  the hypothesis that we're putting  forward is that that  would actually allow us to be able to  turn a kid who wouldn't have been a good  responder  into a good responder and that for us  was very very exciting  so last thing that next the last thing  that i want to show you  is another study separate study that we  did where we used  oxytocin which is a molecule that  affects social behavior and it's known  to affect the social brain we  gave it to kids with autism and what  we're able to show this is a  double-blind placebo-controlled study  we're able to show that there was a high  degree of overlap between the areas of  the brain  that a single dose of oxytocin  would activate and make more  interconnected  relative to placebo there was a high  degree of overlap between those parts of  the brain and the parts of the brain  that predict  good outcomes and good response to  pivotal response training  and so you can sort of see where we're  going with this is that perhaps we've  got  an approach where imaging can allow us  to use a pharmacological agent a very  targeted way  for kids who don't have the activity  that they need to take part in  pivotal response training successfully  to give it to them to  boost their response to the pivotal  response training for example  and we think this can apply to any  evidence-based intervention but  right now we're actually studying this  with funding from the simons foundation  to see if we can  indeed take those kids who don't have  the activity initially and help boost it  nice to mention that now we've been  talking about  kids broadly and what i'm really talking  about  is is four five six year olds and ab