ism is such a challenging disorder  and i mean one of the things which was  clearly evident is that what we've been  doing  up with just you know a clinical  assessment  up until fairly recently has really been  inadequate to  tell the difference between boys with  autism and girls with autism in  particular  and you mentioned something about the  genes for  girls possibly being different than the  genes for boys  with autism what do we know about the  genetics  of asd in the context of it's it's  clearly not  one gene maybe it's multiple genes may  if they're sex  linked at all perhaps that's explanatory  what do we know about that and  maybe you can explain some of that sure  so  as you mentioned that there's no one  gene that's been discovered that  you know causes autism every time um  when it when it's um different  or present or absent  bigger or smaller but there are  a number of genes that increase the risk  for developing autism  and every time i say the number  it's tomorrow it'll grow but i think the  numbers around  about 100 right now of high confidence  autism candidate genes that separately  or in combination greatly increase the  risk for developing autism  and in addition um so those are kind of  high penetrant genes and then in  addition  there are many many genes that  contribute to risk in sort of the  background  of common genetic variation  that taken in isolation  really don't increase the risk of autism  but when you start  putting certain common genetic variation  together  in certain patterns and then possibly  combine it with  rare genetic variation that is much more  likely to directly cause autism you get  this  this um tapestry  of many genes it could be  hundreds or thousands that then  give you you know a much increased risk  for autism and even then  it's not deterministic so you could have  a lot of autism  risk genes and still not develop autism  you could have  one autism risk gene and develop autism  and so it's it's non-deterministic  and very very complex and then overlay  on that the fact that  some of those genes are sex-linked  and then that some of those  genes play different roles in  brain development depending upon  whether they're in  a boy brain or a girl brain  and the different genetic background of  boys and girls so not necessarily sex  linked per se but  in the milieu of what  genes do depending on whether they're  in the presence of female or male brain  development and the hormonal  milieu going on that  you get an even more complicated picture  so for example  at first blush from our data the genes  that  seem to be hits and candidate genes for  boys with autism and autism in general  are related to synaptic development to  pruning of synapses  to glial development as well  whereas in girls with autism  a lot of what we're seeing in terms of  the gene modules gene expression modules  that we're finding  seem to be more involved with  inflammatory response  and um and control and regulation of  that inflammatory response  that's super interesting so as we kind  of  reaching the top of the hour here i'm  curious of your your thoughts on are  there any  data resources that are available for  people who would want to  say take a look at some of these  different data types that you mentioned  and  finally just what's the role of data  science in helping to  make progress understanding autism  disorder  spectrum disorders yeah yeah sure so  you know one data resource of course is  our network  and you know since we're the data  coordinating center  and jack you're the lead of that  being able to collaborate on that data  get access to the de-identified data and  utilize it for different projects  is fairly straightforward in addition  there's more broadly what's called the  well i call it indar jack remind me of  what it's now  called was the the nih data archive  thank you thank you used to be called  the national database for autism  research and it was autism  focused now it